1. Real-Time Chat Application Backend
A real-time chat application requires a backend with WebSocket communication to handle messages between users. TypeScript ensures type safety in defining user and message structures.


typescript

// Install dependencies: npm install express ws @types/ws
import express from "express";
import { WebSocketServer } from "ws";

interface ChatMessage {
  user: string;
  message: string;
  timestamp: Date;
}

const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// WebSocket server
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true });

wss.on("connection", (ws) => {
  ws.on("message", (data) => {
    const chatMessage: ChatMessage = JSON.parse(data.toString());
    console.log(`Received: ${chatMessage.message} from ${chatMessage.user}`);
    
    // Broadcast to all connected clients
    wss.clients.forEach((client) => {
      if (client !== ws && client.readyState === 1) {
        client.send(JSON.stringify(chatMessage));
      }
    });
  });
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${PORT}`);
});
Highlights:

ChatMessage interface enforces the structure of chat messages.
Ensures proper typing for WebSocket communication.
2. E-Commerce Product Search
A TypeScript-powered backend API for product search with filtering and sorting.

Code:

typescript

// Install dependencies: npm install express
import express, { Request, Response } from "express";

interface Product {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  category: string;
}

const products: Product[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Laptop", price: 1200, category: "Electronics" },
  { id: 2, name: "Chair", price: 200, category: "Furniture" },
  { id: 3, name: "Headphones", price: 150, category: "Electronics" },
];

const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// Product search route
app.get("/search", (req: Request, res: Response) => {
  const { category, minPrice, maxPrice } = req.query;

  let result = products;

  if (category) {
    result = result.filter((p) => p.category === category);
  }

  if (minPrice) {
    result = result.filter((p) => p.price >= Number(minPrice));
  }

  if (maxPrice) {
    result = result.filter((p) => p.price <= Number(maxPrice));
  }

  res.json(result);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});
Highlights:

Query parameters are dynamically filtered using TypeScript's type enforcement.
Provides a scalable way to add more search criteria.


3. Employee Management Dashboard
A real-time dashboard to display employee data with TypeScript and Angular on the frontend.

Code (Angular Frontend):

typescript
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

interface Employee {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  role: string;
  salary: number;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-employee-dashboard',
  template: `
    <h1>Employee Dashboard</h1>
    <ul>
      <li *ngFor="let employee of employees">
        {{ employee.name }} - {{ employee.role }} (${{ employee.salary }})
      </li>
    </ul>
  `,
})
export class EmployeeDashboardComponent implements OnInit {
  employees: Employee[] = [];

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.http.get<Employee[]>('/api/employees').subscribe((data) => {
      this.employees = data;
    });
  }
}
Highlights:

Employee interface ensures type safety for API responses.
Angular's HttpClient leverages TypeScript to avoid runtime errors.
